      <h1>How Much Is My Personal Injury Case Worth?</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p>How much is my personal injury case worth? There is no single answer. A California injury claim is worth what your records prove for medical care and lost income, plus what a jury would pay for pain, reduced by your share of fault, and capped by the insurance that actually exists. Most "average settlement" pages mention only the first.</p>
<p>Anyone quoting a figure before reading your records and the policy is guessing. You can build a rough estimate yourself.</p>
<h2 id="what-decides-how-much-your-personal-injury-case-is-worth">What decides how much your personal injury case is worth</h2>
<p>Every injury claim comes down to four inputs:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Economic damages.</strong> Medical bills, future care, lost wages, lost earning capacity, out-of-pocket costs.</li><li><strong>Non-economic damages.</strong> Pain, suffering, loss of enjoyment, the effect on your sleep, work and family.</li><li><strong>Your share of fault.</strong> California reduces your recovery by your percentage of responsibility.</li><li><strong>Available insurance.</strong> The policy limits behind the person who hurt you, plus any coverage of your own that applies.</li></ul>
<p>Change one and the answer moves, sometimes by six figures. A figure copied from another case tells you almost nothing.</p>
<p>In order, the arithmetic looks like this:</p>
<ol><li>Add accepted medical bills, likely future care, lost earnings and out-of-pocket costs. That is your economic total.</li><li>Add non-economic damages. Adjusters often start at 1.5 to 3 times the medical total, then move up or down on treatment length, surgery and how much daily life changed.</li><li>Multiply by one minus your share of fault. At 20% fault, multiply by 0.8.</li><li>Cap the result at the total insurance available.</li></ol>
<p>Most estimates stop at step two. Steps three and four are where the real number appears.</p>
<h3 id="what-moves-a-case-up-or-down">What moves a case up or down</h3>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on value</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Steady treatment, no gaps</td><td>Raises it</td><td>Therapy twice weekly for four months, notes every visit</td></tr><tr><td>Weeks-long gap in care</td><td>Lowers it</td><td>Insurer says you healed in March and returned in June</td></tr><tr><td>Objective findings: fracture, tear, surgery</td><td>Raises it</td><td>Surgeon recommends a discectomy, records support it</td></tr><tr><td>Soft tissue strain resolving in six weeks</td><td>Lowers it</td><td>Good for you, small for the file</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-existing condition in the same area</td><td>Lowers it, not to zero</td><td>You still recover for aggravation of the old injury</td></tr><tr><td>Dashcam, witness, citation</td><td>Raises it</td><td>Report shows the other driver cited at the scene</td></tr><tr><td>Delay in reporting the crash or pain</td><td>Lowers it</td><td>First neck complaint logged three weeks later</td></tr><tr><td>Your percentage of fault</td><td>Lowers it proportionally</td><td>25% fault removes a quarter of the total</td></tr><tr><td>Limits below the damages</td><td>Caps it</td><td>$109,000 in damages, $30,000 policy, $30,000 ceiling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>On pre-existing conditions: insurers argue that a 2019 back injury makes your 2026 injury worthless. California law does not work that way. You are compensated for making an existing condition worse. It changes the number, but it does not erase the claim.</p>
<h2 id="economic-damages-what-the-paperwork-can-prove">Economic damages: what the paperwork can prove</h2>
<p>Start here. This is the part with receipts.</p>
<p><strong>Past medical care.</strong> ER treatment, imaging, surgery, therapy, prescriptions. One California quirk matters: for past care, recovery is generally tied to what providers accepted, not what they billed. A $40,000 bill your health plan negotiated to $12,000 usually supports something near $12,000, plus your out-of-pocket share. If you were uninsured or paid cash, billed amounts can still matter, so keep every bill.</p>
<p><strong>Future medical care.</strong> Ongoing therapy, a surgery you will likely need, or lifetime care. This is where a treating doctor's narrative earns its cost. "Will likely require a fusion within five years" changes the math.</p>
<p><strong>Lost wages.</strong> Pay stubs, tax returns, an employer letter. If you drive for a living or work gigs, bank deposits and app records do the same job.</p>
<p><strong>Lost earning capacity.</strong> Different from lost wages. It is the hit to what you can earn going forward, like a warehouse worker with a back injury who can no longer lift. Usually needs a vocational expert.</p>
<p><strong>Out-of-pocket costs.</strong> Mileage, parking, rideshares, home help, equipment.</p>
<p>Keep every receipt, even small ones. The paperwork folder is often worth more than a dramatic story.</p>
<h2 id="non-economic-damages-and-the-multiplier-myth">Non-economic damages and the multiplier myth</h2>
<p>Pain and suffering is compensable in California. There is no formula in the statute and no published rate card.</p>
<p>You will read about the "multiplier." An adjuster multiplies your medical specials by 1.5 to 3, sometimes higher, to reach a pain and suffering figure. It is a negotiating habit, not law. Nothing requires an insurer to use it.</p>
<p>Here is why it breaks down. Two people each have $50,000 in bills after a rear-end crash. One finished therapy in ten weeks and is back at work. The other had surgery and still cannot sit through a full day. Same specials, different cases. A formula cannot tell them apart, so a seasoned adjuster does not really use one. They look at treatment length, surgery, scarring, and what changed in your daily life.</p>
<p>Non-economic damages track how much your life changed and how well the file proves it. Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-pain-and-suffering-damages-are-calculated/">how pain and suffering damages are calculated</a> covers the factors.</p>
<h2 id="comparative-fault-the-percentage-that-eats-the-check">Comparative fault: the percentage that eats the check</h2>
<p>California follows pure comparative fault. You can recover even if you were mostly responsible, but your award drops by your share. 20% fault means a 20% cut.</p>
<p>A worked example. You are stopped on Van Ness. A driver on a phone rear-ends you. Your brake lights were out.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Past medical (accepted amounts)</td><td>$38,000</td></tr><tr><td>Future physical therapy</td><td>$12,000</td></tr><tr><td>Lost wages</td><td>$9,000</td></tr><tr><td>Pain and suffering</td><td>$50,000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total before fault</strong></td><td><strong>$109,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The other insurer argues 35% fault because of the brake lights. Your lawyer argues 10%. They settle at 20%. That is $21,800 off, so you collect $87,200.</p>
<p>At trial, if a jury said 40%, the same injuries produce $65,400. Same crash, same records, one number different, $21,800 gone.</p>
<p>Fault is not a side issue. It is a line item. Our post on <a href="/blog/what-happens-if-you-were-partially-at-fault-for-an-accident/">what happens if you were partially at fault</a> explains how those arguments get made.</p>
<h2 id="policy-limits-the-ceiling-almost-nobody-mentions">Policy limits: the ceiling almost nobody mentions</h2>
<p>This is the gap in nearly every "average settlement" article.</p>
<p>A claim can be worth $200,000 and collect $30,000, because $30,000 is all the insurance that exists. California requires at least $30,000 per person and $60,000 per accident in bodily injury liability, plus $15,000 for property damage, on policies issued or renewed since January 1, 2025. The <a href="https://www.insurance.ca.gov/01-consumers/105-type/95-guides/01-auto/">California Department of Insurance</a> publishes the current minimums.</p>
<p>So every case has two numbers:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Case value.</strong> What a jury would award if the defendant had unlimited money.</li><li><strong>Collectible value.</strong> What you can actually reach.</li></ol>
<p>Beyond the at-fault driver's policy, look for:</p>
<ul><li>Your own uninsured and underinsured motorist coverage. California insurers must offer it and you can only decline it in writing, so it may already be on your policy. Check the declarations page, not what the sales pitch promised.</li><li>Medical payments coverage, which pays bills regardless of fault.</li><li>A commercial policy, if the driver was working. Delivery vans and rideshare vehicles often carry more.</li><li>A second defendant. The car's owner. A bar that served a drunk driver. A landlord who left a hazard.</li><li>A public entity, if a dangerous road or crosswalk played a role. Short clock on those.</li></ul>
<p>Your own underinsured motorist coverage often fills that gap. If an offer arrives, read <a href="/blog/why-you-should-never-accept-the-first-insurance-settlement-offer/">why you should never accept the first settlement offer</a> before signing.</p>
<h2 id="from-settlement-to-money-in-hand">From settlement to money in hand</h2>
<p>A settlement figure is not a bank deposit. Three things come off the top, in this order:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Attorney fees.</strong> A contingency percentage of the gross recovery, per your written agreement.</li><li><strong>Case costs.</strong> Filing fees, records, experts, depositions. Some firms advance them and repay from the recovery; others bill as you go. Read which.</li><li><strong>Medical liens.</strong> Hospitals, ambulance services and doctors sometimes assert a lien against your recovery, and your health plan may claim reimbursement.</li></ul>
<p>A $100,000 settlement with a one-third fee, $6,000 in costs and a $12,000 lien nets about $48,700. Ask any lawyer to walk you through that subtraction before you sign.</p>
<h2 id="why-published-averages-mislead">Why published averages mislead</h2>
<p>Search "average personal injury settlement California" and you find tidy figures. Almost none explain where they came from.</p>
<p>An average blends a fender bender with a wrongful death case, counts only settled claims, and hides the ones dismissed or tried. Then the deeper problem: settlements pile up at round numbers like $30,000, $50,000 and $100,000 because those are policy limits. The median is shaped by the insurance people bought, not by how badly they were hurt.</p>
<p>So a statewide average describes the insurance market. It says little about your neck.</p>
<p>Ask instead:</p>
<ul><li>What are my total specials, and how much was actually paid?</li><li>Is liability clear, and what is my fault exposure?</li><li>What policies exist, and what are the limits?</li><li>What will a doctor say about future care?</li></ul>
<p>Those four answers produce a range you can defend. A number from a marketing page does not.</p>
<h2 id="what-to-do-in-the-first-two-weeks">What to do in the first two weeks</h2>
<p>Evidence disappears fast. Footage overwrites, witnesses move, bruises fade.</p>
<ol><li>Get examined and follow the plan. Treatment gaps are the most common way a claim shrinks.</li><li>Photograph the scene, the vehicles, your injuries, whatever caused the fall. Same day.</li><li>Get the police or incident report number.</li><li>Collect witness names, and ask nearby businesses in writing to preserve camera footage.</li><li>Notify your own insurer and check for uninsured motorist and medical payments coverage. Decline a recorded statement.</li><li>Start a symptom log: sleep, pain level, what you could not do.</li><li>Do not post about the crash. Do not sign a release or cash a check.</li></ol>
<p>One deadline sits over all of it. Under <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CCP&amp;sectionNum=335.1">Code of Civil Procedure § 335.1</a>, California gives you two years from the injury to file a lawsuit. Miss it and the claim is usually gone. Claims against a city, county, or other public entity run on a much shorter clock: generally six months to present a government claim under the California Government Claims Act before you can sue. Small omissions cause most of the damage, so read <a href="/blog/common-mistakes-that-can-reduce-your-personal-injury-settlement/">the common mistakes that reduce settlements</a> before you talk to an adjuster.</p>
<h2 id="the-honest-answer">The honest answer</h2>
<p>Your case is worth a range, not a number, and the range comes from records, fault and insurance. Nobody can give you a real figure over the phone. A lawyer can pull the coverage, read the medicine, and tell you where the range starts and what would move it.</p>
<p>Sulfab Law Office reviews injury cases from our office at 369 Pine Street in San Francisco. For a straight read on your situation, call (415) 360-5266. You can also read about <a href="/personal-injury-lawyer-san-francisco/">personal injury representation in San Francisco</a> first.</p>
<p>This is general information, not legal advice about your case. Deadlines and coverage differ, and the details matter.</p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="how-much-is-my-personal-injury-case-worth-in-california">How much is my personal injury case worth in California?</h3>
<p>There is no fixed amount. Value depends on proven medical costs and lost income, what your pain and changed daily life are worth, your percentage of fault, and the available insurance limits. Fault and coverage often matter more than the diagnosis.</p>
<h3 id="what-is-the-average-personal-injury-settlement-in-california">What is the average personal injury settlement in California?</h3>
<p>There is no reliable public average. Settlements are private and each case differs. Published figures mix minor and catastrophic claims, exclude dismissed and tried cases, and cluster around policy limits. Treat a statewide figure as marketing. Your records are the only real starting point.</p>
<h3 id="is-the-pain-and-suffering-multiplier-real">Is the pain and suffering multiplier real?</h3>
<p>It is a negotiation convention, not a legal rule. Adjusters sometimes multiply medical specials by 1.5 to 3, but nothing requires it. Insurers weigh treatment duration, surgery, scarring, and how much daily life changed. A formula cannot capture those differences, which is why it often fails in practice.</p>
<h3 id="how-do-personal-injury-lawyers-charge-in-california">How do personal injury lawyers charge in California?</h3>
<p>Most personal injury lawyers in California work on a contingency fee. You pay no hourly fee up front, and the fee comes out of the recovery as a percentage. That percentage varies by firm and typically rises if the case goes to trial. Costs like filing fees and experts may be separate, so read the written agreement.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-still-recover-if-i-was-partly-at-fault">Can I still recover if I was partly at fault?</h3>
<p>Yes. California uses pure comparative fault, so you can recover even if you were mostly responsible, with the award reduced by your share. At 20% fault, a $100,000 claim becomes $80,000. Insurers often open by inflating your share, so crash evidence matters as much as the records.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-the-driver-has-no-insurance-or-minimum-coverage">What if the driver has no insurance or minimum coverage?</h3>
<p>You may still recover through your own uninsured or underinsured motorist coverage, up to the limits you bought. Insurers must offer it, and declining it requires a written waiver, so check your declarations page. If the driver was working, a commercial policy may also apply. Cases with no coverage at all are the hardest.</p>
<h3 id="how-long-does-a-personal-injury-case-take">How long does a personal injury case take?</h3>
<p>It varies. A clear-liability crash with soft tissue injuries can resolve in a few months. Disputed fault, surgery, or a policy limit fight often pushes past a year. A lawsuit adds discovery and trial scheduling. The two-year deadline is the outer boundary, and most cases settle before it.</p>
<h3 id="how-much-will-i-get-after-lawyer-fees-and-medical-bills">How much will I get after lawyer fees and medical bills?</h3>
<p>Less than the settlement figure. The fee comes off the gross recovery as a percentage, case costs are usually repaid from it, and hospitals or health plans may assert liens. Ask for a written estimate of each deduction before you sign. A $100,000 settlement can net closer to $48,000 once fees, costs and liens are paid.</p>
